[gd_resource type="Environment" load_steps=2 format=3 uid="uid://cq2gubwharfenv"]

; Environment for Lantern Wharf. The blue hour on a wet dock: the sun is a
; flattened orange disc on the west-south-west wall with ten minutes left in it,
; the four sodium floods on the wall corners have taken over as the key light,
; and the mist that came up the mole with the tide is lying in the bottom metre
; of the yard (D-009, D-056).
;
; It was a dusk map in name only before this. The sun sat 17 degrees up at 0.9
; energy, which is a mid-afternoon key by any measure; the floods at 5.0 under
; it were four lamp heads that happened to glow; and the air between them was a
; vacuum. Everything below is in service of the opposite: the sun grazes, the
; floods do the work, and the air is a medium.
;
;   * The sky is `wharf_sky.tres` — the island's shader, this map's numbers.
;   * **Ambient is half sky and half a stated colour**, which no other map here
;     does. With `ambient_light_sky_contribution` at 1.0 the whole yard went the
;     colour of the sunset: the sun's halo and the afterglow band dominate the
;     radiance cubemap at this hour, so every shaded face came out orange and
;     the map's own argument — warm lit face, cool shaded face, which is how you
;     read the corner of a box at a glance — stopped being true. At 0.5, mixed
;     against a dusk blue, the shaded faces go indigo and the lit ones stay
;     sodium.
;   * **`background_energy_multiplier` is the exposure control** (see
;     `rust_env.tres`, which measured it). Set from rendered frames, not taste.
;     The three spawn-pad renders come out at 0.00%, 0.00% and 0.04% of pixels
;     crushed below 0.02 luminance and 0.38%, 0.85% and 0.03% clipped; the map
;     before this pass clipped 3.0% of pad 0 on bare container paint, which is
;     what a mid-afternoon sun on signal red does.
;   * **Volumetric fog is on**, and this is the only map in the game where it
;     is. Four spots at 13.5 m aimed into a 36 m box is the best case for it
;     here: short beams, crossing in the middle, landing on wet concrete. But
;     it is on at **0.011, a third of the island's 0.032**, and that number was
;     not chosen either — it is what the readability measurement allowed. On the
;     map's longest ground sightline (23.4 m, and 25.0 m from a box top, both
;     from `tools/parkour_report`) a Bog-sized target at 25 m reads against its
;     background at a Michelson contrast of **0.24 with the fog on and 0.52
;     with it off**. Half the contrast is a real price and it buys the halo
;     round every lamp, the beam a flood throws down into the mist, and a middle
;     distance that is no longer empty; four times that price, which is where
;     the island's density lands, buys a yard you cannot fight in.
;     `volumetric_fog_length` is 48 m rather than 96: the far corner is 51 m
;     away, so half the length is twice the froxel resolution for the same cost.
;     `ambient_inject` is nearly off (0.04) on purpose — fog lit by ambient is
;     an even wash, and the whole point of a beam is that the medium around it
;     is dark.
;   * **Screen-space reflections are on**, which no other map here uses either.
;     They are the wet dock. The floor is a flat upward plane covering half of
;     every frame, the brightest things in the scene are lamp heads above it,
;     and SSR is what turns those into the long vertical smears that say "this
;     concrete is standing in water".
;
;     Both fade distances are set against an artefact rather than to taste, and
;     it is the same artefact twice: a screen-space technique fails where the
;     screen runs out, and a floor is where it runs out. `ssr_fade_out` at the
;     3 m it was first given stopped the reflections dead in a straight
;     horizontal line across the middle of every frame. At 28 m that went, and a
;     fainter version of it came back along the bottom edge, where a near floor
;     pixel's reflected ray leaves the screen behind the camera and there is
;     nothing to march into — measured at a 0.040 jump in mean row luminance at
;     row 1378 of 1440. `ssr_fade_in` at 3.5 m softens the ray's first metres
;     enough that the jump falls to 0.0007, which is the same number the scene
;     gives with SSR switched off entirely, and it costs 2% of the bright
;     reflected pixels in the lower half of the frame. Worth it twice over.
;   * Distance fog is thinner than it was (0.0035) because the volumetric fog
;     now does that job, and `fog_sun_scatter` is down from 0.35 to 0.12 for the
;     same reason the ambient was split: it was painting the whole west half of
;     the map orange.
;   * Glow threshold stays at Rust's 2.0. The lamp heads are unshaded emissive
;     at 9x, the quay lamps at 5x and the sun disc at 7x, so those bloom and
;     nothing lit by them does.

[ext_resource type="Sky" path="res://resources/config/wharf_sky.tres" id="1_wharf_sky"]

